Hershey Fonts (Roman Simplex capitals A-Z)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Used in: src/features/spare/glyphs/hersheyRomanSimplex.ts
Source:  rowmans.jhf, records 501-526, from
         https://github.com/kamalmostafa/hershey-fonts (hershey-fonts/rowmans.jhf);
         identical to the first Usenet distribution of the Hershey Fonts
         (mod.sources, volume 4, issue 42, 1986, submitted by Pete Holzmann).
Change:  converted from James Hurt's character-pair format into TypeScript
         polylines with numeric coordinates (x from the left side bearing,
         y downwards from the baseline), by
         scripts/generate-hershey-glyphs.js (npm run glyphs:generate).
         No glyph shape was altered. The acknowledgements below are also kept
         as a legal comment in the built JavaScript, next to the glyph data.

Acknowledgements required by the distribution notice:

  - The Hershey Fonts were originally created by Dr. A. V. Hershey while
    working at the U. S. National Bureau of Standards.
  - The format of the Font data in this distribution was originally created by
        James Hurt
        Cognition, Inc.
        900 Technology Park Drive
        Billerica, MA 01821
        (mit-eddie!ci-dandelion!hurt)

Use restriction of the distribution, reproduced as published:

  This distribution of the Hershey Fonts may be used by anyone for
  any purpose, commercial or otherwise, providing that:
    1. The following acknowledgements must be distributed with
       the font data:
       - The Hershey Fonts were originally created by Dr.
         A. V. Hershey while working at the U. S.
         National Bureau of Standards.
       - The format of the Font data in this distribution
         was originally created by
           James Hurt
           Cognition, Inc.
           900 Technology Park Drive
           Billerica, MA 01821
           (mit-eddie!ci-dandelion!hurt)
    2. The font data in this distribution may be converted into
       any other format *EXCEPT* the format distributed by
       the U.S. NTIS (which organization holds the rights
       to the distribution and use of the font data in that
       particular format). Not that anybody would really
       *want* to use their format... each point is described
       in eight bytes as "xxx yyy:", where xxx and yyy are
       the coordinate values as ASCII numbers.

The converted data is not in the NTIS format.

